The Customer Service uses separate consumer groups for `UserRegistered` and `UserActivated` events from the same topic (`identity.user.events`), causing both groups to consume all messages and filter in-memory—effectively processing every message twice.

## Changes

**KafkaConsumerConfig.kt**
- Removed duplicate consumer factory beans (`userActivatedConsumerFactory`) and container factories (`userActivatedKafkaListenerContainerFactory`)
- Single `consumerFactory()` now uses base group ID for all listeners
- Single `kafkaListenerContainerFactory()` shared across event types

**UserActivatedConsumer.kt**
- Updated `@KafkaListener` annotation to reference shared `kafkaListenerContainerFactory`

Both consumers now operate within the same consumer group. Kafka delivers each event to exactly one listener, which filters by event type. This eliminates redundant message consumption while preserving event routing logic.

```kotlin
// Before: Separate consumer groups
fun consumerFactory(): ConsumerFactory<String, String> {
    return createConsumerFactory("$baseGroupId-user-registered")
}
fun userActivatedConsumerFactory(): ConsumerFactory<String, String> {
    return createConsumerFactory("$baseGroupId-user-activated")
}

// After: Single shared consumer group
@Bean
fun consumerFactory(): ConsumerFactory<String, String> {
    val configProps = mapOf(
        ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG to baseGroupId,
        // ... other config
    )
    return DefaultKafkaConsumerFactory(configProps)
}
```

